01-10T09:14:31Z","commit_id":"a37ee647be226068ea7152cc04bdfd22f4694bcb"},{"id":3664384120,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7aahB4","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 11**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Due to the large number of review comments, Critical, Major severity comments were prioritized as inline comments.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn @.gitmodules:\n- Around line 4-9: Update .gitmodules to use standard HTTPS repository URLs\ninstead of SSH aliases: replace the FDD submodule URL\ngit@github.com-personal:ainetx/FDD.git with https://github.com/ainetx/FDD.git\nand replace the GTS submodule URL git@github.com:GlobalTypeSystem/gts-spec.git\nwith https://github.com/GlobalTypeSystem/gts-spec.git so both guidelines/FDD and\nguidelines/GTS match the existing DNA submodule’s HTTPS style and avoid\nrequiring special SSH host aliases.\n\nIn `@modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-categories/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 317-352: The schema mixes hard deletes and soft deletes: the\ncategories.parent_id FK uses ON DELETE CASCADE while the categories.state column\nsupports a DELETED value and the \"Delete Category\" algorithm implements\nsoft-delete semantics; remove the ON DELETE CASCADE from the parent_id foreign\nkey and update delete logic to perform application-level soft deletes (set state\n= 'DELETED' and cascade to children via the Delete Category routine), ensuring\nuniqueness constraints (e.g., uq_category_name_parent) and indexes still behave\ncorrectly for soft-deleted rows; alternatively, if you truly want hard deletes,\nremove the DELETED state and convert the Delete Category algorithm to perform\nphysical deletes, but prefer the first option per the design notes.\n- Around line 138-155: The Assign Entity to Category flow must validate that the\nentity's domain type matches the category's domain type before constructing the\nJSON Patch and sending PATCH to /gts/{entity_id}; modify the algorithm to\nfetch/inspect both entity.domain_type and category.domain_type (or their type\nidentifiers, e.g., category.v1~widget) after step 1 and if they differ return a\n400/409 error with a clear message (do not proceed to the patch), otherwise\ncontinue with constructing the patch, updating cache/UI, and returning the\nupdated entity.\n\nIn\n`@modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-query-capabilities/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 38-39: The DESIGN.md currently references a non-existent schema\npath\n`../../../gts/types/hypernetix/hyperspot/ax/query_capabilities.v1.schema.json`;\nupdate that link to the correct existing schema at\n`../../../gts/types/query/v1/query_capabilities.schema.json` so the reference in\nthe \"References from `gts/types/`\" section points to\n`query_capabilities.schema.json`.\n\nIn `@modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-reporting/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 896-911: The schema for report_generations declares a foreign key\nreferencing report_layouts(id) which doesn't exist; either create the referenced\nreport_layouts table with an id column of type VARCHAR(500) (to match the FK) in\nthe feature-report-layouts schema, or remove the FOREIGN KEY (report_id)\nREFERENCES report_layouts(id) line from the report_generations CREATE TABLE\nstatement so the relationship is enforced only in application logic; update\nmigrations/schema accordingly and ensure report_id column type remains\nVARCHAR(500) to keep compatibility with either choice.\n- Around line 268-294: The snippet is missing its function signature and initial\nlogic; add an async function named register_schedule with signature async fn\nregister_schedule(report: &Report, ctx: &SecurityCtx) -> Result<()> that first\nchecks report.settings.schedule.enabled and returns Ok(()) if false, then\nconstructs a SchedulingJob (id format \"analytics.report.{report.id}\", cron,\ntimezone, callback_url \"/api/v1/reports/{id}/generate\", and metadata as json!\nwith report_id, recipients, format), call\nplatform_client.scheduling.create_job(&job, ctx)? to register it, and finally\nreturn Ok(()); reference symbols: register_schedule, Report, SecurityCtx,\nSchedulingJob, platform_client.scheduling.create_job, and json! for metadata.\n- Around line 237-263: The snippet starts mid-function; add a complete async\nfunction signature and body for generate_report(report_id: &str, format:\nExportFormat, filters: Option<Filters>, ctx: &SecurityCtx) ->\nResult<GenerationResult> that performs steps 1–4 (call load_report_config,\nvalidate_dependencies, await execute_queries_parallel(&report.widgets) and\ncapture query_results), map query_results into rendered_widgets used by\ncompose_report(&report, &rendered_widgets), then call export_report, upload via\nstorage.upload, and return a populated GenerationResult (generation_id,\nStatus::Completed, download_url, expires_at); ensure all referenced helpers\n(load_report_config, validate_dependencies, execute_queries_parallel,\ncompose_report, export_report, storage.upload) are invoked and errors\npropagated.\n\nIn `@modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-schema-values/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 424-455: Replace the simple {\"error\": \"...\"} example responses in\nthe Values Schema error scenarios with RFC 7807 Problem Details objects: for\neach case (Values Schema Not Found, Invalid Value Type, Duplicate Field Names,\nSchema In Use, Version Conflict, Unauthorized Access) provide a JSON body\ncontaining type (unique problem URI), title (short summary), status (HTTP code)\nand detail (specific message), and move any extra context fields (schema_id,\nallowed_types, selector_count, current_version, required_role) into extension\nmembers of the problem object; also update the fallback notes to state that\nfield-level validation errors should be returned as a Problem Details object\nwith embedded field errors and that schema-fetch failures during rendering\nreturn an empty list while logging a Problem Details entry for diagnostics.\n\nIn\n`@modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-tenancy-enablement/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 436-500: The DESIGN.md contains fragmented pseudocode blocks\n(e.g., the enableForTenants algorithm, the for reference_id loops, calls to\nenable_entity_for_tenants, transaction.commit(), and the gts_registry.exists()\nMissingReference error) that lack function signatures and context and duplicate\nSection C; either remove these incomplete fragments or replace them with\ncomplete, self-contained examples: provide full function signatures and\nsurrounding context for enableForTenants/enable_entity_for_tenants including\nparameter types and return value, show the transactional commit/rollback flow\naround transaction.commit(), and present the validation snippet with the\nMissingReference error type definition; alternatively, delete these blocks and\nadd a clear cross-reference to the canonical algorithm in Section C to avoid\nduplication.\n- Around line 146-208: The pseudocode fragments for the transitive dependency\nresolver and reference extractor are incomplete and self-referential; replace\nthem with two complete, self-contained algorithm specifications: (1) provide a\nfull function signature and I/O contract for fn\nenable_entity_for_tenants(entity_id: &GtsIdentifier, tenants: &[TenantId], ctx:\n&SecurityCtx) -> Result<Vec<GtsIdentifier>, Error>, include initialization\n(start transaction), load & validate entity via gts_registry.get, idempotent\nupdate of entity.enabled_for (merge/union with tenants), collect\nenabled_entities, call extract_references(&entity) to get reference IDs,\nrecursively call enable_entity_for_tenants for each reference while preventing\ncycles (visited set) and checking existing enablement to skip work,\ncommit/rollback transaction and return the list of newly enabled IDs; (2)\nprovide a complete signature for fn extract_references(entity: &Entity) ->\nResult<Vec<GtsIdentifier>, Error> with explicit handling for all entity types\n(\"datasource\", \"widget\", \"dashboard\", \"report\", default), null/optional fields,\ndeduplication, and error cases; ensure edge cases are addressed (missing\nentities, permission checks via ctx, transitive cycles, partial failures with\nrollback) and reference the symbols enable_entity_for_tenants,\nextract_references, gts_registry, enabled_for, and transaction.commit/rollback\nso reviewers can locate and implement the logic.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/analytics/src/api/rest/gts_core/error_handler.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-153`: **Consider using `modkit::api::problem` for RFC 7807 error handling.**\n\nThis custom `ProblemDetails` implementation duplicates functionality that already exists in the framework. Using the shared implementation ensures consistency across modules and reduces maintenance burden.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/analytics/src/domain/gts_core/identifier.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-26`: **Use the parsed `GtsID` result instead of manual string parsing.**\n\nThe `GtsID` is parsed for validation (line 10) but the result is discarded. The subsequent manual string splitting on `~` (lines 12-23) duplicates parsing logic that should ideally come from the `gts` crate.\n\nConsider using `GtsID`'s API to extract the type identifier portion rather than re-implementing the parsing logic manually. This would ensure consistency with the crate's semantics and reduce maintenance burden.\n\n\n\n\n\n```web\ngts crate Rust documentation type identifier extraction\n```\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🟡 Minor comments (18)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-schema-values/DESIGN.md-186-189 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`186-189`: **Clarify role hierarchy for \"or higher\" permission checks.**\n\nThe algorithms state \"analytics.developer or higher role\" (line 186) and \"analytics.viewer or higher role\" (line 252), but the document does not explicitly define what \"higher\" means or establish a role hierarchy. The Permission Matrix lists three roles (viewer, developer, admin) without indicating their precedence. Explicitly document the role ordering in the access control section (e.g., admin > developer > viewer) or replace \"or higher\" with specific enumerated roles.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-schema-values/DESIGN.md-392-398 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`392-398`: **Document cross-feature dependency on values-selector-templates.**\n\nThe dependency check query at lines 392-398 references the `gts_templates` table, which belongs to the feature-values-selector-templates feature (owns GTS type `template.v1~values_selector.v1~`). This table is not defined in this design document and the dependency is missing from the Dependencies section (line 5).\n\nUpdate the Dependencies section to include `feature-values-selector-templates` and document the expected structure of the `gts_templates` table or reference where it's defined in that feature's design.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>guidelines/GTS-1-1 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **Add documentation explaining why the GTS submodule is needed.**\n\nThe GTS submodule is correctly configured in `.gitmodules` with a valid commit hash pointing to `github.com:GlobalTypeSystem/gts-spec.git`. However, the PR description and codebase contain no documentation explaining the purpose of this submodule or how it fits into the FDD-based analytics implementation. Please add context to the PR description or relevant design documentation to clarify:\n\n- What GTS (Global Type System) provides for this project\n- Why it's necessary as a git submodule vs. other dependency management approaches\n- How it integrates with the FDD analytics implementation\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/analytics-sdk/Cargo.toml-7-7 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`7-7`: **Use workspace inheritance for `thiserror` to maintain consistency.**\n\nReplace `thiserror = \"2.0\"` with `thiserror = { workspace = true }` to match the pattern used throughout the workspace (libs/modkit, modkit-security, etc.). This ensures all crates use the same version definition from the workspace manifest.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-dashboards/DESIGN.md-296-317 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`296-317`: **Incomplete/malformed code block in validation algorithm.**\n\nThe code block appears to be incomplete or corrupted. Lines 308-316 contain what looks like Rust code fragments (`let capabilities = ...`, `validate_odata_params(...)`) that appear outside a proper code fence, making the section difficult to read and potentially confusing.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\nEither complete the pseudocode block with proper formatting:\n\n```diff\n **Steps**:\n\n 1. Load query from GTS registry\n 2. Verify query enabled for tenant\n 3. Load template from GTS registry\n 4. Validate schema compatibility (query returns vs template expects)\n 5. Validate OData params against query capabilities\n 6. **RETURN** validation result\n-    let capabilities = gts_registry.get(&query.capabilities_id)?;\n-    validate_odata_params(&widget.datasource.params, &capabilities)?;\n-    \n-    // 4. Validate template config against schema\n-    let config_schema = gts_registry.get(&template.config_schema_id)?;\n-    validate_json_schema(&widget.template.config, &config_schema)?;\n-    \n-    Ok(())\n-}\n-```\n+\n+```rust\n+fn validate_widget_config(widget: &Widget) -> Result<()> {\n+    let query = gts_registry.get(&widget.datasource.query_id)?;\n+    let template = gts_registry.get(&widget.template.template_id)?;\n+    let capabilities = gts_registry.get(&query.capabilities_id)?;\n+    validate_odata_params(&widget.datasource.params, &capabilities)?;\n+    \n+    let config_schema = gts_registry.get(&template.config_schema_id)?;\n+    validate_json_schema(&widget.template.config, &config_schema)?;\n+    \n+    Ok(())\n+}\n+```\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/FEATURES.md-656-678 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`656-678`: **Feature count inconsistency in GTS Type Distribution.**\n\nLine 678 states \"26 GTS types + 5 runtime/business features = **21 features**\" but the document header (line 3) and the actual feature list show **22 features**. Additionally, the section header says \"26 types → 20 features\" while the text says 21 features.\n\nPlease reconcile these numbers. The feature list shows 22 distinct features numbered 1-22.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-## GTS Type Distribution (26 types → 20 features)\n+## GTS Type Distribution (26 types → 22 features)\n```\n\nAnd line 678:\n```diff\n-**Total**: 26 GTS types + 5 runtime/business features = 21 features\n+**Total**: 26 GTS types across 17 type-owning features + 5 runtime/business features = 22 features\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>guidelines/FDD-Adapter/specs/api-contracts.md-11-12 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-12`: **Inconsistent OpenAPI specification locations.**\n\nThere's a discrepancy in the documented OpenAPI file locations:\n- Line 11: `docs/api/api.json` (JSON format)\n- Line 35: `modules/*/architecture/openapi/v1/api.yaml` (YAML format)\n\nThis could cause confusion about which is the source of truth. Clarify whether:\n1. YAML is the source and JSON is generated, or\n2. These serve different purposes (design-time vs runtime)\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 35-39\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-datasources/DESIGN.md-208-235 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`208-235`: **Orphaned code fragment in UI algorithm section.**\n\nSimilar to the previous algorithm section, this has orphaned Rust code (lines 222-234) with closing braces and function calls that appear after the pseudocode steps. The document should either complete the code example or remove the orphaned fragments.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-datasources/DESIGN.md-189-200 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`189-200`: **Orphaned code fragment in algorithm section.**\n\nThe \"Parameter Merging\" algorithm section has orphaned Rust code lines (195-200) that appear after the pseudocode steps but before the closing code fence. This looks like a copy-paste artifact where part of a code example was left dangling.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix - remove orphaned code or complete the example</summary>\n\nEither remove the orphaned lines:\n```diff\n 4. Apply user inputs (highest priority)\n 5. **RETURN** merged parameters\n-    // Apply user runtime inputs (highest priority)\n-    final_params.merge(user_inputs);\n-    \n-    final_params\n-}\n ```\n```\n\nOr restructure to include a complete code example in a separate fenced block.\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-datasources/DESIGN.md-488-495 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`488-495`: **Orphaned closing brace after example.**\n\nLines 493-494 contain an orphaned `}` and code fence closing that don't belong to any code block. This appears to be a copy-paste artifact.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n - Final merged: `{$filter: \"status eq 'active' and region eq 'EMEA'\", $top: 100}`\n-}\n-```\n\n ---\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-query-values/DESIGN.md-500-525 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`500-525`: **Error response format inconsistent with other features.**\n\nThis design uses a custom error format:\n```json\n{\"error\": \"...\", \"code\": \"NOT_FOUND\"}\n```\n\nHowever, other feature designs (dashboard-layouts, plugins) specify RFC 7807 format with `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, and `instance` fields. Consider aligning with RFC 7807 for API consistency.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 RFC 7807 format example</summary>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"https://example.com/problems/not-found\",\n  \"title\": \"Query Values Not Found\",\n  \"status\": 404,\n  \"detail\": \"Query values for query_id 'xyz' not found\",\n  \"instance\": \"/api/analytics/v1/gts/{id}\"\n}\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-plugins/DESIGN.md-171-200 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`171-200`: **Algorithm section has orphaned code fragments.**\n\nLines 183-199 contain Rust code that appears after the algorithm's numbered steps without a proper code block opening. This creates a confusing mix of prose algorithm and implementation code.\n\nEither:\n1. Remove the orphaned code and keep pure algorithm prose, or\n2. Wrap the code in a proper fenced block with context explaining it's an implementation example\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>guidelines/FDD-Adapter/specs/tech-stack.md-10-11 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-11`: **Add explicit MSRV constraint to align documentation with project configuration.**\n\nThe tech-stack.md documents \"Latest stable (1.70+)\" but the project's Cargo.toml workspace lacks a `rust-version` field, and rust-toolchain.toml specifies only the \"stable\" channel without a pinned version. To prevent drift and ensure toolchain consistency, add `rust-version = \"1.70\"` to the workspace package in `./Cargo.toml`.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-schema-query-returns/DESIGN.md-200-204 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`200-204`: **Clarify whether arrays of scalars are allowed in the validation algorithm.**\n\nThe service algorithm for Create Schema (lines 200-204) validates that field types must be scalar, returning 400 if not. However, the acceptance criteria at line 522 states \"Arrays of scalars allowed.\" The algorithm doesn't account for this case—it would reject array types.\n\nConsider updating the algorithm to explicitly handle `array<scalar_type>` validation, or clarify the acceptance criteria if arrays are not actually supported.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 520-523\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-schema-query-returns/DESIGN.md-534-536 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`534-536`: **Broken anchor reference.**\n\nThe reference `#actor-analytics-developer` doesn't exist in this document. The defined actor anchor is `#actor-dashboard-designer` (line 53). Update the reference to point to the correct anchor.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n **References**:\n-- [Section B: Actor Flow - Create Schema](`#actor-analytics-developer`)\n+- [Section B: Actor Flow - Create Schema](`#actor-dashboard-designer`)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/analytics/src/api/rest/gts_core/routes.rs-97-114 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`97-114`: **PATCH endpoint missing request body schema.**\n\nThe PATCH endpoint (lines 97-114) does not specify a JSON request body schema via `.json_request::<...>()`, unlike POST (line 66) and PUT (line 85). According to the description, this endpoint accepts JSON Patch operations (RFC 6902), but the OpenAPI spec won't document the expected request body format.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Consider adding request body schema</summary>\n\n```diff\n     router = OperationBuilder::patch(\"/analytics/v1/gts/{id}\")\n         .operation_id(\"gts_core.patch_entity\")\n         .summary(\"Partially update GTS entity\")\n         .description(\"Apply JSON Patch to GTS entity (restricted to /entity/* paths)\")\n         .tag(\"GTS Core\")\n         .path_param(\"id\", \"GTS entity identifier\")\n         .public()\n+        .json_request::<JsonPatchDto>(openapi, \"JSON Patch operations (RFC 6902)\")\n         .handler(handlers::patch_entity)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/architecture/features/feature-query-execution/DESIGN.md-299-306 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`299-306`: **Incomplete code block in JWT Plugin Hook section.**\n\nThe code block starting at line 299 (`**Plugin Hook**: before_jwt_sign(ctx, claims)`) is opened but there's no actual code inside—it appears to be truncated. The block immediately transitions to the **Important** section. Either complete the code example or remove the empty block markers.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Plugin Hook**: `before_jwt_sign(ctx, claims)`\n-```\n \n **Important**: \n```\n\nOr provide a complete example:\n\n```diff\n **Plugin Hook**: `before_jwt_sign(ctx, claims)`\n+\n+```rust\n+fn before_jwt_sign(ctx: &PluginCtx, claims: &mut Claims) -> Result<(), PluginError> {\n+    // Add custom claims\n+    claims.insert(\"custom_scope\", \"analytics:read\");\n+    Ok(())\n+}\n+```\n \n **Important**: \n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/analytics/analytics/src/api/rest/gts_core/handlers.rs-185-192 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`185-192`: **Use 400 Bad Request instead of 501 when required filter is missing.**\n\nWhen `base_type` is `None`, the handler returns `501 NOT_IMPLEMENTED` with the message \"base type filter is required for routing.\" If a filter is a required input, the appropriate status is `400 BAD_REQUEST`, not `501`. `501` implies the server doesn't support the functionality, not that the client provided invalid input.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        None => Err(Problem::new(\n-            StatusCode::NOT_IMPLEMENTED,\n-            \"Not Implemented\",\n-            \"List endpoint declared; base type filter is required for routing in this deployment\",\n-        )\n-        .with_instance(\"/analytics/v1/gts\")\n-        .with_code(\"NOT_IMPLEMENTED\")),\n+        None => Err(Problem::new(\n+            StatusCode::BAD_REQUEST,\n+            \"Missing Required Filter\",\n+            \"A base type filter (e.g., startswith(id, 'prefix')) is required for listing entities\",\n+        )\n+        .with_instance(\"/analytics/v1/gts\")\n+        .with_code(\"MISSING_BASE_TYPE_FILTER\")),\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/175#pullrequestreview-3664384120","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/175","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/175#pullrequestreview-3664384120"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/175"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-15T08:15:56Z","commit_id":"2c8fb7d5db13a4e9275d857e299a059105b90ed0"}]},{"source_pr_number":173,"source_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/173","source_state":"closed","source_author":"nanoandrew4","target_issue_number":null,"title":"[PR #173] Remove humantime_serde crate in favor of stdlib duration parsing","status":"exported","mirrored_at":null,"comments_status":"none","comments_mirrored":0,"pr_head_ref":"remove-humantime-serde","pr_head_sha":"b310a696678d4ec33a6f6490ab8a9f8e8d068b1b","pr_base_ref":"main","pr_head_owner":"nanoandrew4","pr_from_fork":1,"source_data":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/173","id":3134916479,"node_id":"PR_kwDOPaLyBs662wd_","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/173","diff_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/173.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/173.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/173","number":173,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Remove humantime_serde crate in favor of stdlib duration parsing","user":{"login":"nanoandrew4","id":14365320,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzY1MzIw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14365320?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4","html_url":"https://github.com/nanoandrew4","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nan